#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb7dde31-bde1-58e8-ad71-ec6b2e8b2319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca9aaad-54c8-5fad-9a03-14113c01dc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb7dde31-bde1-58e8-ad71-ec6b2e8b2319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451311} :answer-field/id #uuid "e741fb79-46a4-593a-9d56-3df9c7384c94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452503} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cc96c4-9bd0-5ec2-9c67-70c12fe56748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} {:answer/id #uuid "71be77cb-f2a0-5da9-a71b-0d54bf22920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05cc96c4-9bd0-5ec2-9c67-70c12fe56748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451313} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fda6962-d111-5d52-9d97-94bb5cf1b992" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "757e1e41-d4bd-5b02-ae6c-09acb0b026a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451315} {:answer/id #uuid "ae88a715-f3a8-55f4-890a-5a6165ebc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae88a715-f3a8-55f4-890a-5a6165ebc0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451316} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19a4f3d-993f-546f-aa3d-c187f50b1a7a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb8cc465-a001-5ad5-bfcd-11eb17855f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-140844" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is not diagonalizable\n- **II.** $A$ must have $4$ integer eigenvalues\n- **III.** If  $\\alpha $ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with the multiplicity $2,$ then the dimension of the eigenspace for the eigenvalue $\\alpha $ is $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, so, $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily integers.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the theorem, the dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36379c9-48d8-5cb1-80f0-4b648453256c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378060} {:answer/id #uuid "600f54d6-7fa1-5cb7-8b0b-961230b3ef4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc06545-06d9-5a6d-b04a-bdc1e2c72d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378062} {:answer/id #uuid "f04919e1-818b-5e94-8009-e3cff20a8da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378063} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a25f6-1c57-57ce-9ba5-04288080446d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "600f54d6-7fa1-5cb7-8b0b-961230b3ef4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378061} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6818491-5219-59f7-95e1-4f023133c8d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eab64778-c05e-560d-a0b4-afdd1f4a716c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95706" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} {:answer/id #uuid "3b601ff3-863f-5502-84d6-13f00e2ad45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395055} {:answer/id #uuid "84da8f15-dc10-55f3-97d6-434ca1ff643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395056} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb077ea-553a-5aa6-a64a-7a2b84621b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\begin{bmatrix}80 & 60 \\\\ 60 & 45\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395057} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2d006a-6b5c-519d-a0ac-32bc5de9d2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a65a271-e819-5161-9c5f-b4658866c25c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395054} :answer-field/id #uuid "3211a6bb-133a-5e40-90d8-fc361c03ff47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05f92170-0d3f-5d3a-aa67-7dea2e387ec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95710" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b8923f-7afa-5e14-b149-d06f52309cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366686} {:answer/id #uuid "02cd176f-d7d8-56e4-9747-5567e658dda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366687} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0c2bac-5abf-5014-936e-ed7fb7b084ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366688} {:answer/id #uuid "970025c4-617f-501a-a55b-b16b187ad5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366689} {:answer/id #uuid "e3545f21-098a-50f3-a494-4ea653f16e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3545f21-098a-50f3-a494-4ea653f16e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366690} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8b9480-d64b-50e7-a54e-52af09ad2e90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61a2391d-9048-50a5-89de-abedafa32ffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-95720" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
